  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Other than some patchy drizzle/fog early this morning, \nexpect today will be a dry day with skies becoming partly sunny. \nWinds will subside as high pressure builds across the region. Highs \nwill be seasonable though on the chilly side as most spots will top \nout in the 50s. \n\nThings become much more interesting thereafter as an upper trough \ndigs into the Great Lakes tonight into Friday, eventually swinging \nover our region on Saturday. The associated surface low will lift \nthrough the St Lawrence Valley, then move east-northeast through the \nCrown of Maine and toward the Canadian Maritimes. Moisture will \nstream northward on deep southwest flow ahead of the trough, \nresulting in increasing clouds tonight. Given the dry airmass that \nwill be in place, rain will be a little slower to develop, but \nexpect widespread precipitation to develop by daybreak Friday. A \npotent 55+ kt jet wrapping around the surface low at 850 mb will \nmove overhead on Friday, leading to very windy conditions atop \nmountain summits; gusts of 55-65 mph are likely. Winds at lower \nlevels are a bit harder to determine, but note that several of the \nCAMs indicate we could see a some breaks in the rain mid-day Friday \nwith the main swath of rain lifting north of the international \nborder. This means less potential hindrance for mixing, increasing \npotential of gusts of 30 mph or more at lower elevations. The \nChamplain Valley would be particularly susceptible to strong gusts \ndue to channeling of the south winds up the Valley. Note that the \n15/00z HREF indicates 85-100% probabilities of gusts greater than 40 \nmph along Lake Champlain and the eastern side of the Champlain \nValley, from the international border down toward Hinesburg and \nBristol. The northern slopes of the northern Greens have similar \nchances of strong winds, with 90+% in locations such as Newport, \nMontgomery, and Glover. Have bumped up gusts with this forecast \npackage, though have kept them below Wind Advisory level (46 mph). \nHowever, it looks like some places could get close to needing an \nAdvisory; subsequent shifts will need to monitor trends closely. \n\nThe surface low pressure slides by just to our north Friday \nafternoon, allowing us to nudge into the 50s to close to 60F. \nHowever, once the low moves to our east, its attendant cold front \nwill quickly sweep through our area, bringing much colder \ntemperatures later Friday and Friday night. The incoming airmass \nwill also be quite dry, but expect there will be enough moisture \naround to squeeze out a bit of mountain snow later Friday night into \nSaturday morning before precipitation ends Saturday afternoon. With \ntemperatures dropping into the mid 20s to mid 30s by Saturday \nmorning, even the lower elevations could see a few flakes, though \nany accumulation looks to remain limited to 1500 ft and above. Even \nthere, though, expect an inch at most given the decreasing moisture \nand relatively warm ground. \n\nSaturday will see decreasing clouds and much colder temperatures. \nHighs will only reach into the mid 30s to low 40s, and expect the \nsummits to remain in the teens and 20s through the day. Winds will \nstill be gusty though not quite as strong as Friday. However, \nthey'll be enough to produce wind chills in the teens and 20s \nSaturday morning, with the higher summits seeing wind chills in the \nsingle digits. Anyone who plans to be recreating in the mountains \nshould prepare for wet, windy, and bitterly cold conditions Friday \nthrough Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure over the mid-Atlantic is expected to \nstrengthen Sunday night and Monday, expanding into New England to \nstart the work week. Global deterministic models differ on the exact \nplacement of this high pressure, which could have some minor impacts \nfor coastal New England, but up in northern New York and Vermont, \ndry weather seems very likely Sunday night through at least next \nThursday despite a couple week frontal boundaries that swing through \nthe region. High temperatures each day should remain around \nseasonable values in the mid 50s to lower 60s. Nights will remain on \nthe chilly side with lows in the upper 20s to lower 40s due to the \nclearer nights and decoupled boundary layer winds under high \npressure.",
